WHAT YOU ALREADY KNOW   What you already know
Chapter 1 classified a currency movement as market risk, a kind of financial risk. Chapter 7 measured Orontes's net euro exposure at 2,000,000 − 400,000 = 1,600,000 and hedged three quarters of it. Chapter 8 computed a value at risk on that position. This chapter explains what the exposure is, and Chapter 11 covers the instruments.
10.1  Rates, and the prices of goods
An exchange rate is the price of one currency in another, and like any price it can be quoted either way round. For a company reporting in dollars, a direct quote states how many dollars one unit of the foreign currency costs — $1.0800 to the euro — and an indirect quote states how many euros a dollar buys, which is 1 ÷ 1.0800 = 0.9259. Both describe the same rate, and reversing them is the single commonest arithmetic error in this topic. Read the quote before computing anything.
The first outcome asks how foreign currency affects the prices of goods and services, and the mechanism runs in both directions.
What the company's product costs a foreign buyer. Orontes's jar sells at $12.00. At 1.0800 dollars to the euro, a European buyer pays 12.00 ÷ 1.0800 = 11.11 euros. If the rate moves to 1.1500 — the euro buying more dollars — the same jar costs 12.00 ÷ 1.1500 = 10.43 euros. Orontes has not changed its price and its product has become 6 per cent cheaper in Europe.
What a foreign input costs the company. A spare part priced at 50 euros costs 50 × 1.0800 = 54.00 before the move and 50 × 1.1500 = 57.50 after it. Orontes's costs have risen without any supplier raising a price.
Three consequences follow and they are the examinable substance.
A weaker home currency helps exporters and hurts importers, and a stronger one does the reverse. Orontes, which exports tahini and imports equipment, is affected both ways and the net position depends on the balance of the two.
Competitiveness changes even where the company does not trade at all. A domestic producer competing with imports is helped by a weaker home currency, because the imports become dearer; it has a currency exposure without a single foreign transaction, which is the economic exposure of section 10.4.
And prices do not adjust fully or immediately. Exporters often hold foreign prices steady and absorb the movement in their margins, to protect market share — pricing to market — which converts a price effect into a profit effect. Orontes's decision whether to pass the euro movement on is exactly Book 3's Chapter 11 question about market structure, asked in another currency.
