10.2  What moves a rate
The outcome asks for the variables that affect exchange rates, and seven cover the syllabus. Learn them with their direction, because a question usually asks which way the rate moves.
Relative inflation. A country whose prices rise faster than its trading partners' sees its currency weaken, because its goods become uncompetitive until the rate corrects. This is purchasing power parity, which says that the expected change in the rate equals the difference in inflation rates. Where home inflation is 8 per cent and the other country's is 3 per cent, the home currency is expected to weaken by about 1.08 ÷ 1.03 = 1.0485, which is 4.85 per cent. Purchasing power parity holds poorly over months and reasonably well over years, which is exactly the opposite of what a treasurer needs.
Relative interest rates. Higher interest rates attract capital and strengthen a currency in the short run, while the forward market prices the opposite: interest rate parity says the currency with the higher interest rate trades at a forward discount, so that no risk-free profit exists from borrowing in one currency and lending in the other. At a spot of 1.0800, a dollar rate of 6 per cent and a euro rate of 3 per cent, the forward rate is 1.06 ÷ 1.03 = 1.0291 times the spot, which is 1.0800 × 1.0291 = 1.1114.
The balance of payments. A persistent trade deficit means more of the home currency is being sold to buy foreign goods than is being bought, which weakens it, subject to capital flows running the other way.
Economic growth and relative performance. A growing economy attracts investment and strengthens its currency, though growth also pulls in imports, which works the other way.
Government and central bank action. Intervention in the market, capital controls, and above all monetary policy. A central bank raising rates to control inflation is acting on the rate whether or not that is its purpose.
Political stability and country risk. The political risk of Chapter 1, priced. Instability, conflict, a disputed election or an unpredictable legal system all drive capital out and weaken a currency.
Expectations and speculation. Rates move on what the market expects rather than on what has happened, which is why an announcement moves a rate and the event it announces often does not. Speculative flows dwarf trade flows in the major currencies, so a rate can move a long way from anything a trade-based model would predict, and stay there.
Two cautions belong with the list, and strong answers include them.
The variables interact and often conflict. Higher interest rates strengthen a currency; the inflation they are being used to fight weakens it. Which dominates depends on what the market believes about the future, not on either variable alone.
And exchange rate forecasting is unreliable over the horizons companies care about. That is not a reason to ignore the variables, which explain the direction of pressure; it is a reason to manage exposure with the instruments of Chapter 11 rather than to take positions based on a view.
