10.4  Three kinds of exposure
Before computing the profit or loss on a transaction, it is worth separating three exposures, because they are measured differently, managed differently and confused constantly.
Transaction exposure. The risk that the home-currency value of a committed foreign-currency cash flow changes between the commitment and the settlement: an invoice issued, an order placed, a loan to be repaid. It is specific, dated, measurable, and it is what the hedges of Chapter 11 address. Section 10.5 computes it.
Translation exposure, also called accounting exposure. The risk that the reported value of foreign assets, liabilities and results changes when they are translated into the reporting currency for the financial statements. It affects the balance sheet and the reported figures without any cash moving, which is why companies disagree about how far it should be hedged: hedging an accounting number with a real cash instrument converts a book entry into a cash gain or loss.
Economic exposure, also called operating exposure. The risk that the present value of the company's future cash flows changes because of a rate movement — including the cash flows of a business that has no foreign transactions at all. It is the largest of the three, the hardest to measure, and the one that cannot be hedged with a forward contract, because it has no date and no amount. It is managed structurally: where production is located, where inputs are bought, what currency prices are set in, how flexible sourcing is.
Figure F810-02 sets them out.
Figure F810-02. Three exposures
@table
Exposure | What is at risk | Horizon | Managed by
Transaction | Committed foreign-currency cash flows | Days to months | Forwards, options, matching
Translation | Reported amounts on consolidation | Reporting date | Matching assets with local borrowing
Economic | Present value of future cash flows | Years | Location, sourcing, pricing, flexibility
@end
