LANGUAGE FOCUS   "Strong", "weak", "up" and "down"
No vocabulary in finance causes more confusion than this one, because the words are relative and the direction depends on which currency you are standing in. "The rate went up" is meaningless without saying which rate: 1.0800 to 1.1500 is up if you are quoting dollars per euro and down if you are quoting euros per dollar, and it is the same event. Strong and weak are safer, because they attach to a named currency — "the euro strengthened" cannot be misread — and professional writing uses them for exactly that reason. English also carries a value judgment in the pair: strong sounds good and weak sounds bad, which is wrong for an exporter, and newspapers in every language make the same mistake. Arabic ارتفاع قيمة العملة and انخفاض قيمتها state the rise and fall of a named currency's value, which is unambiguous; French appréciation and dépréciation do the same. In an exam answer, name the currency and say strengthened or weakened, then give the percentage and the base.
