Chapter summary
Figure F810-04 brings the chapter together.
Figure F810-04. Chapter 10 summary map: the key rule for each section.
An exchange rate is the price of one currency in another and can be quoted directly or indirectly, with 1 ÷ 1.0800 = 0.9259 the same rate read the other way; it reaches the price of goods in both directions, so a 12.00 jar costs 12.00 ÷ 1.0800 = 11.11 euros before a move and 12.00 ÷ 1.1500 = 10.43 after it, a weaker home currency helps exporters and hurts importers, and a purely domestic producer competing with imports has an exposure without a single foreign transaction. Rates move on relative inflation — purchasing power parity putting the expected change at 1.08 ÷ 1.03 = 1.0485, or 4.85 per cent — relative interest rates, which interest rate parity turns into a forward of 1.0800 × 1.0291 = 1.1114, the balance of payments, growth, government and central bank action, political stability, and expectations, with the variables interacting and forecasting unreliable over the horizons that matter. One movement gives two percentages: from 1.0800 to 1.1500 the euro appreciated 0.0700 ÷ 1.0800 = 6.48 per cent while the dollar depreciated 0.0563 ÷ 0.9259 = 6.08 per cent, because the base changes. Three exposures must be kept apart — transaction, on committed flows; translation, on reported amounts with no cash moving; and economic, on the value of future cash flows, which has no date and so cannot be forwarded away. And the profit or loss on a cross-border transaction is the movement applied to the amount between recording and settlement: 2,300,000 − 2,160,000 = 140,000 of gain on the receivable, 460,000 − 432,000 = 28,000 of loss on the payable, a net 140,000 − 28,000 = 112,000 — which is a windfall on an unmanaged position rather than a performance, and would have been a loss of similar size had the euro moved the other way.
