Item | Answer
Euro appreciation | 6.48 per cent: 0.0700 ÷ 1.0800 = 6.48 per cent.
Dollar depreciation | 6.08 per cent: 0.0563 ÷ 0.9259 = 6.08 per cent.
A 12.00 jar in euros after the move | 10.43 euros: 12.00 ÷ 1.1500 = 10.43.
Gain on the receivable | 140,000: 2,300,000 − 2,160,000 = 140,000.
Loss on the payable | 28,000: 460,000 − 432,000 = 28,000.
Forward rate by interest parity | 1.1114: 1.0800 × 1.0291 = 1.1114.
What it tells you | The first three are about competitiveness and the last three about cash — and the exam asks for both, because a movement that helps the income statement may be hurting the business, or the reverse.
@end
Multiple-choice questions
P10-01  Answer A. Home currency per foreign unit.
B is wrong. That is the indirect quote.
C is wrong. Forwards are a different rate.
D is wrong. Inflation is a driver, not a quote.
P10-02  Answer A. 12.00 ÷ 1.1500 = 10.43 euros.
B is wrong. That multiplies instead of dividing.
C is wrong. That uses the earlier rate.
D is wrong. The price is in dollars.
P10-03  Answer A. Exports cheaper abroad, imports dearer at home.
B is wrong. That is a stronger currency.
C is wrong. Competitiveness changes directly.
D is wrong. Imports become more expensive.
P10-04  Answer A. Inflation differentials.
B is wrong. That is interest rate parity.
C is wrong. Growth is separate.
D is wrong. Volumes are a consequence.
P10-05  Answer A. Interest differentials.
B is wrong. That is purchasing power parity.
C is wrong. Trade is a separate variable.
D is wrong. Political risk is separate.
P10-06  Answer A. 1.0800 × 1.0291 = 1.1114.
B is wrong. That uses the wrong differential.
C is wrong. That applies the rates directly.
D is wrong. That is the ratio, not the rate.
P10-07  Answer A. 0.0700 ÷ 1.0800 = 6.48 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the dollar's figure.
C is wrong. That is the absolute change, not a percentage.
D is wrong. That misplaces a decimal.
P10-08  Answer A. 0.0563 ÷ 0.9259 = 6.08 per cent.
B is wrong. That is the euro's figure.
C is wrong. That is the absolute change in the inverse rate.
D is wrong. That has no basis in the calculation.
P10-09  Answer A. Consolidation of foreign amounts.
B is wrong. Settlement gives transaction exposure.
C is wrong. Competition gives economic exposure.
D is wrong. A forward is a hedge.
P10-10  Answer A. Structural choices.
B is wrong. Forwards need a date and an amount.
C is wrong. So do options.
D is wrong. So do swaps.
P10-11  Answer A. 2,300,000 − 2,160,000 = 140,000.
B is wrong. The direction is favourable.
C is wrong. That halves it.
D is wrong. That is the recorded amount.
P10-12  Answer A. 140,000 − 28,000 = 112,000.
B is wrong. The net is a gain.
C is wrong. This adds instead of subtracting.
D is wrong. That is the loss alone.
Case-style set: the European year
What happened. The euro strengthened from 1.0800 to 1.1500 dollars, an appreciation of 0.0700 ÷ 1.0800 = 6.48 per cent, which is the same movement as a dollar depreciation of 0.0563 ÷ 0.9259 = 6.08 per cent. Orontes held euro receivables and euro payables through that movement, gaining 2,300,000 − 2,160,000 = 140,000 on the first and losing 460,000 − 432,000 = 28,000 on the second, for a net 140,000 − 28,000 = 112,000.
Is it good news? It is a favourable outcome and not a good result. Nothing the company did produced it: the position was unhedged, so an identical movement in the other direction would have produced a loss of comparable size, and the board should be told that explicitly. A gain of this kind belongs in other income, as it has been reported, and should never be described as a trading improvement. The useful conclusion is not that 112,000 was earned but that the company was carrying 1,600,000 of net exposure that nobody had decided to carry.
The distributor's request. The distributor is right about the arithmetic and wrong about the entitlement. At the new rate the jar costs 12.00 ÷ 1.1500 = 10.43 euros against 11.11 before, so Orontes's product has become about 6 per cent cheaper in Europe without any price change. But that is a benefit the movement handed to the distributor, not a saving Orontes made — Orontes still receives 12.00 — and the question is who keeps it. Three answers are defensible: hold the euro price and let the distributor's margin improve, in exchange for a volume commitment; cut the euro price and take the share gain; or share it, with a contractual mechanism that adjusts the price when the rate moves more than a stated amount in either direction. The third is the one I would propose, because it also protects Orontes when the rate moves back.
What should change. The policy of Chapter 6 — at least 75 per cent of contracted exposure hedged — should actually be applied, which it evidently was not. The natural offset of euro purchases should be taken first, leaving the net 1,600,000 to hedge. And the economic exposure, which is the real one, belongs in the board's discussion of where Orontes buys its packaging and in what currency it prices its exports.
